    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4566",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In comparison to Homo habilis, what gave Homo erectus the ability to migrate so far, so fast?\n\nOptions: \nA. microcephaly\nB. larger brain size\nC. ability to swim\nD. ability to communicate\nE. stronger muscles\nF. enhanced vision\nG. neoteny\nH. longer lifespan\nI. bipedalism\nJ. intelligence\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "MiniMax-M3",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Looking at this question, I need to identify the key adaptation that enabled *Homo erectus* to migrate out of Africa and across vast distances much more successfully than *Homo habilis*.\n\n*Homo erectus* was the first hominin to leave Africa and spread across Asia and Europe. The key biological change that facilitated this rapid, extensive migration was a significant increase in brain size. A larger brain provided greater cognitive abilities for problem-solving, planning long journeys, adapting to new environments, creating tools, and coordinating group activities—all essential for surviving migration into unfamiliar territories.\n\nWhile *Homo habilis* had a brain size of around 600-650 cc, *Homo erectus* had a brain size of approximately 800-1000+ cc, representing a substantial leap in cognitive capacity that enabled more sophisticated behaviors needed for long-distance migration.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 169,
        "output_tokens": 176,
        "total_tokens": 345
      },
      "provider": "minimax",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": 0.0005237999999999999,
    "accuracy": 0.0,
    "for_optimality": false
  },
