e rethink - this is about cumulative inventory management across segments:\n\n```python\ndef solve():\n    N, M = map(int, input().split())\n    S = input().strip()\n    \n    logo = 0  # logo shirts owned\n    \n    for ch in S:\n        if ch == '0':\n            # Reset: all worn shirts become clean\n            # But we don't track worn/clean - just track owned\n            pass\n        elif ch == '1':\n            # Meal: plain or logo\n            if M > 0:\n                M -= 1  # use plain\n            else:\n                logo += 1  # buy logo\n        elif ch == '2':\n            # Contest: logo only\n            logo += 1\n    \n    print(logo)\n\nsolve()\n```\n\nWait, this doesn't account for washing. Let me think again...\n\nActually the issue is more subtle. Let me re-examine: we have M plain shirts available. When we use one, it's dirty. When we hit a '0', all dirty shirts become clean again.\n\nBut we don't need to track plain shirts explicitly - we can always assume we use a fresh plain shirt up to M times, and beyond that, we need logos.\n\nLet me check sample 1 with this: `\"112022\"`, M=1\n- '1': M=1>0, M=0, no logo\n- '1': M=0, logo=1\n- '2': logo=2\n- '0': reset\n- '2': logo=3\n- '2': logo=4\n\nResult: 4. Still wrong!\n\nThe issue: when M=0 and we need a shirt for meal, we buy a logo. But this logo gets \"used up\" (dirty) until next 0. If multiple consecutive meals happen, we still only need 1 logo (it just stays dirty).\n\nSo I should track *dirty* logo shirts too:\n\n```python\ndef solve():\n    N, M = map(int, input().split())\n    S = input().strip()\n    \n    logo_total = 0\n    logo_clean = 0\n    plain_clean = M\n    \n    for ch in S:\n        if ch == '0':\n            logo_clean = logo_total  # all owned logos become clean\n            plain_clean = M\n        elif ch == '1':\n            if plain_clean > 0:\n                plain_clean -= 1\n            else:\n                logo_total += 1\n                logo_clean += 1\n        elif ch == '2':\n            logo_total += 1\n            logo_clean += 1\n    \n    print(logo_total)\n\nsolve()\n```\n\nLet me trace sample 1: `\"112022\"`, M=1\n- logo_total=0, logo_clean=0, plain_clean=1\n- '1': plain_clean=0, used plain\n- '1': plain_clean=0, logo_total=1, logo_clean=1\n- '2': logo_total=2, logo_clean=2\n- '0': logo_clean=2, plain_clean=1\n- '2': logo_total=3, logo_clean=3\n- '2': logo_total=4, logo_clean=4\n\nStill 4. Hmm.\n\nLet me look at the expected answer more carefully. Day 1: logo, Day 2: plain, Day 3: logo, Day 4: wash, Day 5: logo, Day 6: logo.\n\nSo days 5 and 6 use the same 2 logos from days 1 and 3! After washing on day 4, those logos are clean again.\n\nIn my trace, on day 5 I'd have logo_total=2, logo_clean=2 (after wash). Use 1 → logo_clean=1. Day 6: use 1 → logo_clean=0. But logo_total stays 2!\n\nThe bug: when using a clean logo, I increment logo_total, but I shouldn't! logo_total should only increment when I *buy*.\n\n```python\ndef solve():\n    N, M = map(int, input().split())\n    S = input().strip()\n    \n    logo_total = 0\n    logo_clean = 0\n    plain_clean = M\n    \n    for ch in S:\n        if ch == '0':\n            logo_clean = logo_total\n            plain_clean = M\n        elif ch == '1':\n            if plain_clean > 0:\n                plain_clean -= 1\n            else:\n                if logo_clean > 0:\n                    logo_clean -= 1\n                else:\n                    logo_total += 1\n        elif ch == '2':",
