ame component.\n    # Actually, the problem states that initially there is NO path between x_i and y_i.\n    # This means comp[x_i] != comp[y_i] for all i.\n    \n    # For each query (p, q), we add an edge between p and q.\n    # The new graph G' is good if for all i=1..K, there is still no path between x_i and y_i.\n    # Adding an edge between p and q merges the connected components containing p and q.\n    # Let C_p = comp[p], C_q = comp[q].\n    # If C_p == C_q, adding the edge doesn't merge any new components (it's within the same component).\n    #   In this case, the connectivity between any two nodes remains exactly the same.\n    #   Since originally x_i and y_i were disconnected, they remain disconnected.\n    #   So the graph is Good.\n    # If C_p != C_q, the edge merges component C_p and C_q into a single component.\n    #   Let the merged component be C_new.\n    #   The graph is NOT good if there exists some i such that x_i and y_i become connected.\n    #   x_i and y_i become connected if and only if one is in C_p, the other is in C_q.\n    #   Specifically, if (comp[x_i] == C_p and comp[y_i] == C_q) or (comp[x_i] == C_q and comp[y_i] == C_p).\n    \n    # To answer queries efficiently, we can pre-calculate for each pair of components (c1, c2)\n    # whether there is a forbidden pair (x_i, y_i) connecting them.\n    # However, K and num_components can be large (up to 2*10^5).\n    # A direct map of (min(c1,c2), max(c1,c2)) -> count or boolean might work.\n    \n    # Let's store for each unordered pair of components {c1, c2}, whether there is a constraint violation.\n    # We only care if there is AT LEAST ONE pair (x_i, y_i) such that {comp[x_i], comp[y_i]} == {c1, c2}.\n    \n    # Map: frozenset({c1, c2}) -> True if there is a path requirement between these two components.\n    # Actually, since we just need to check existence, a set of tuples (min_c, max_c) is sufficient.\n    \n    bad_pairs = set()\n    \n    for i in range(K):\n        cx = comp[queries_x[i]]\n        cy = comp[queries_y[i]]\n        # cx != cy is guaranteed by problem statement\n        if cx > cy:\n            cx, cy = cy, cx\n        bad_pairs.add((cx, cy))\n        \n    # Now process each query\n    results = []\n    \n    for i in range(Q):\n        p = add_edges_p[i]\n        q = add_edges_q[i]\n        \n        cp = comp[p]\n        cq = comp[q]\n        \n        if cp == cq:\n            # Adding edge within same component doesn't change connectivity between components\n            results.append(\"Yes\")\n        else:\n            # Check if merging cp and cq creates a path between any forbidden pair\n            if cp > cq:\n                cp, cq = cq, cp\n            \n            if (cp, cq) in bad_pairs:\n                results.append(\"No\")\n            else:\n                results.append(\"Yes\")\n                \n    print('\\n'.join(results))\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    solve()\n```",
