reater[i]) * (nextGreaterOrEqual[i] - j) * (j - prevSmaller[j]) * (nextSmallerOrEqual[j] - j)`.\n  - But `R ≥ nextGreaterOrEqual[i]` and `R ≥ j`, with `R < nextSmallerOrEqual[j]` and `R < nextGreaterOrEqual[i]`. Since `nextSmallerOrEqual[j] > j` and `nextGreaterOrEqual[i] > i`, binding is `max(j, nextGreaterOrEqual[i]) ≤ R < nextSmallerOrEqual[j]`.\n\n**Final formula for i < j** (using `a = prevGreater[i]`, `b = nextGreaterOrEqual[i]`, `c = prevSmaller[j]`, `d = nextSmallerOrEqual[j]`):\n- L-count: `i - max(a, c)`, since `a ≥ c` automatically, this is `i - a`\n- R-count: `min(b, d) - max(j, b) + ... `\n\nLet me verify with the simpler case. Since `b > i` and `b > j` may not both hold:\n- If `b ≤ d`: R-count = `d - b`\n- If `b > d`: R-count = `d - j` (since `j < d < b`)\n\nSo **for i < j**:\n- L-count = `i - a`\n- R-count = `min(d, b) - max(j, b if b≤d else j)` = `min(d, b) - b` if `b ≤ d`, else `d - j`\n\nWait, let me redo this. We need `R` such that:\n- `R ≥ j` and `R ≥ b` → `R ≥ max(j, b)`\n- `R < d` and `R < b`... no, we just need no greater-or-equal in `(i, j]` (already enforced by L,R), and `nums[i]` is the max in `[L,R]`. So `R < b` and no smaller-or-equal in `(j, R]`, meaning `R < d`.\n\nSo `R ∈ [max(j, b), d)`, count = `d - max(j, b)`.\n\nSimilarly, for **i > j**, swap roles:\n- `R_count = j - c` where `c = nextSmallerOrEqual[j]`... \n\nLet me just code the cleaner symmetric version using `left[i]`/`right[i]` for both greater and smaller bounds, treating both orderings:\n\n```python\nclass Solution:\n    def sumOfPower(self, nums: List[int]) -> int:\n        MOD = 10**9 + 7\n        n = len(nums)\n        \n        # Build monotonic stack bounds\n        # pGE[i] = index of previous greater element (exclusive), -1 if none\n        # nGE[i] = index of next greater-or-equal element (exclusive), n if none\n        # pSE[i], nSE[i] similarly for smaller\n        pGE = [-1] * n\n        nGE = [n] * n\n        pSE = [-1] * n\n        nSE = [n] * n\n        \n        stack = []\n        for i in range(n):\n            while stack and nums[stack[-1]] <= nums[i]:\n                stack.pop()\n            pGE[i] = stack[-1] if stack else -1\n            stack.append(i)\n        \n        stack = []\n        for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):\n            while stack and nums[stack[-1]] < nums[i]:\n                stack.pop()\n            nGE[i] = stack[-1] if stack else n\n            stack.append(i)\n        \n        stack = []\n        for i in range(n):\n            while stack and nums[stack[-1]] >= nums[i]:\n                stack.pop()\n            pSE[i] = stack[-1] if stack else -1\n            stack.append(i)\n        \n        stack = []\n        for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):\n            while stack and nums[stack[-1]] > nums[i]:\n                stack.pop()\n            nSE[i] = stack[-1] if stack else n\n            stack.append(i)\n        \n        ans = 0\n        for i in range(n):\n            # i is the position of max = nums[i]\n            # left bound L: pGE[i] < L <= i\n            # i is the position of min = nums[i]  \n            # right bound R: i <= R < nSE[i]\n            for j in range(n):\n                if i == j:\n                    # single element group\n                    L_lo = pGE[i] + 1\n                    L_hi = i + 1\n                    R_lo = i\n                    R_hi = nSE[i]\n                    if L_lo < L_hi and R_lo < R_hi:\n                        ans = (ans + nums[i] * nums[i] * nums[i]) % MOD\n                    continue\n                if i < j:\n                    # nums[j] is min, position j\n                    # L must be in (max(pGE[i], pSE[j]), i] and L <= j\n                    # Since pGE[i] < i < j, and pSE[j] < j, the binding is max(pGE[i], pSE[j])\n                    # Since nums[j] < nums[i], and nums[stack elements]... \n                    # pGE[i] >= pSE[j] because nums[i] > nums[j] and both are on left side\n                    # Actually pGE[i] could be < pSE[j] if there's a smaller element further left than any greater\n                    L_lo = max(pGE[i], pSE[j]) + 1\n                    L_hi = i + 1\n                    # R must be >= j and >= nGE[i], R < nSE[j] and R < nGE[i]\n                    # Wait, R must have nums[i] as max, so no element > nums[i] in [L, R]\n                    # and nums[i] >= nums[j], so nGE[i] is the right bound\n                    # R must have nums[j] as min, so no element < nums[j] in [L, R]\n                    # So R < nSE[j]\n                    R_lo = max(j, nGE[i])\n                    R_hi = min(nSE[j], nGE[i])\n                    # Hmm, R_lo and R_hi need to be consistent\n                    if L_lo < L_hi and R_lo < R_hi:\n                        count = (L_hi - L_lo) * (R_hi - R_lo)\n                        ans = (ans + nums[i] * nums[i] * nums[j] * count) % MOD\n                else:  # i > j\n                    # nums[j] is min, nums[i] is max",
