    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**Solution Explanation**\n\nFor each second we do the same two operations:\n\n* remove the first `k` characters of the current word\n* append **any** `k` characters to the end\n\nOnly the **content** of the removed block matters, because after removal the next\n`k` characters become the prefix of the word.  \nTherefore after the first second the new word is\n\n```\nword = original_word[k … n-1]  +  X                (|X| = k)\n```\n\nwhere `X` is an arbitrary string we may choose.  \nAfter the second second we again remove the first `k` characters, now taken from\n`original_word[k … n-1]`, and we may append another arbitrary `X'`.\n\nThe crucial observation is:\n\n*We can choose the appended characters freely, but they must equal the prefix\nthat will be removed in the next second.*  \n\nIf we want the whole word to be exactly the original one after `t` seconds,\nthe **whole sequence of removed blocks** (each of length `k`) must be a\nrearrangement of the original word, because every removed character must reappear\nsomewhere later as an appended character.\n\nConsequences  \n\n* After `t` seconds we have removed exactly `t·k` characters and also appended\n  exactly `t·k` characters.\n* The word reverts to its original state **iff** the string formed by the\n  removed blocks, read in order, is a *cyclic shift* of the original word by a\n  multiple of `n` positions (where `n = len(word)`).\n\nLet `n = len(word)`.  \nIf we remove blocks `B₁, B₂, …, B_t` (each length `k`), the concatenated removed\nstring is `S = B₁ B₂ … B_t`. After `t` seconds the word equals\n\n```\noriginal_word[k … n-1]  +  B₁ B₂ … B_t            (length = n)\n```\n\nFor this to equal `original_word[0 … n-1]` we need\n\n```\nB₁ B₂ … B_t  =  original_word[0 … k-1]            (the first block of the original)\n```\n\nand the remaining part of the original word must already appear as the suffix.\nIn other words `S` must be a prefix of the original word repeated enough times\nto reach length `t·k`.  \n\nBecause we are free to choose the content of the blocks (as long as they are\nvalid to revert the word), the smallest `t > 0` is the **period** of the\nstring `word` with respect to block size `k`.\n\nFormally: we need the smallest `t > 0` such that the string formed by the first\n`t·k` characters of the infinite repetition of `word` equals the original word.\nEquivalently, `t·k` must be a multiple of `n`, i.e.\n\n```\nt·k  ≡ 0 (mod n)\n```\n\nThe minimal positive `t` satisfying that congruence is\n\n```\nt = n / gcd(n, k)\n```\n\nIf `k` does **not** divide `n`, we still can revert the word – we just need a\nwhole number of full cycles.\n\n*Proof Sketch*\n\n* The operations never change the multiset of characters, only their order.\n* After `t` seconds the whole word is a cyclic shift of the original word by\n  `t·k (mod n)` positions (the shift comes from removing a total of `t·k`\n  characters from the front). The word equals the original one exactly when the\n  shift is a multiple of `n`, i.e. `t·k` is a multiple of `n`.\n* The smallest positive `t` with that property is `n / gcd(n, k)` – a basic\n  number‑theory fact about linear Diophantine equations.\n\nThus the answer is simply\n\n```\nanswer = n // gcd(n, k)\n```\n\nThe algorithm runs in O(n) time for the gcd (Euclidean algorithm) and O(1) extra\nspace.\n\n---\n\n**Reference Implementation (Python 3)**\n\n```python\nfrom math import gcd\n\ndef minimumSecondsToRevert(word: str, k: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns the minimum positive number of seconds needed for `word` to revert\n    to its original state when each second we remove the first `k` characters\n    and append any `k` characters.\n\n    Parameters\n    ----------\n    word : str\n        The initial word (1 <= len(word) <= 50).\n    k : int\n        Length of the block removed/appended each second (1 <= k <= len(word)).\n\n    Returns\n    -------\n    int\n        Minimum seconds (> 0) required.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(word)\n    # After t seconds we have removed t*k characters from the front.\n    # The word reverts when this total shift is a multiple of n.\n    # => smallest t > 0 with t*k % n == 0\n    return n // gcd(n, k)\n```\n\nThe function follows directly from the mathematical derivation above and\nconforms to the required constraints.",
