    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_137",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are at a fruit market with different types of exotic fruits on display.\nYou are given a 1-indexed array prices, where prices[i] denotes the number of coins needed to purchase the i^th fruit.\nThe fruit market has the following offer:\n\nIf you purchase the i^th fruit at prices[i] coins, you can get the next i fruits for free.\n\nNote that even if you can take fruit j for free, you can still purchase it for prices[j] coins to receive a new offer.\nReturn the minimum number of coins needed to acquire all the fruits.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: prices = [3,1,2]\nOutput: 4\nExplanation: You can acquire the fruits as follows:\n- Purchase the 1^st fruit with 3 coins, you are allowed to take the 2^nd fruit for free.\n- Purchase the 2^nd fruit with 1 coin, you are allowed to take the 3^rd fruit for free.\n- Take the 3^rd fruit for free.\nNote that even though you were allowed to take the 2^nd fruit for free, you purchased it because it is more optimal.\nIt can be proven that 4 is the minimum number of coins needed to acquire all the fruits.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: prices = [1,10,1,1]\nOutput: 2\nExplanation: You can acquire the fruits as follows:\n- Purchase the 1^st fruit with 1 coin, you are allowed to take the 2^nd fruit for free.\n- Take the 2^nd fruit for free.\n- Purchase the 3^rd fruit for 1 coin, you are allowed to take the 4^th fruit for free.\n- Take the 4^t^h fruit for free.\nIt can be proven that 2 is the minimum number of coins needed to acquire all the fruits.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= prices.length <= 1000\n1 <= prices[i] <= 10^5",
    "prediction": "MiniMax-M3",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**Solution Explanation**\n\nWe need to decide for each fruit whether to **pay** for it (which also gives us the next `i` fruits for free, where `i` is the 1‑based index of the paid fruit) or to **take it for free** from some previously paid fruit.\n\nThe natural way to model this is dynamic programming over the array from right to left.\n\n---\n\n### Key observation\n\nWhen we are at position `j` (0‑based) we already know the optimal cost for the suffix `prices[j+1:]`.  \nIf we decide to pay for fruit `j` (cost `prices[j]`), we are allowed to take the next `j+1` fruits for free, i.e. we can skip the segment `prices[j+1 … j+1+j]`. The cost after this choice is therefore:\n\n```\nprices[j] + dp[j + (j+1) + 1]\n```\n\nThe first `j+1` is the index shift (1‑based to 0‑based).  \nWe also have the option **not** to pay for `j` now; then we just take it for free from some earlier paid fruit and move to `j+1`:\n\n```\ndp[j+1]\n```\n\nTaking the minimum of these two options gives the optimal cost for the prefix ending at `j`.\n\n---\n\n### DP definition\n\nLet `dp[i]` be the minimum coins needed to acquire all fruits from index `i` to the end (`prices[i:]`).  \nAnswer is `dp[0]`.\n\nTransition:\n\n```\ndp[i] = min(\n    dp[i+1],                               # take fruit i for free\n    prices[i] + dp[i + i + 2]              # pay for fruit i, get next (i+1) fruits free\n)\n```\n\n`i + i + 2 = 2*i + 2` because we skip `i+1` fruits after index `i`.\n\nBase case: `dp[n] = 0` (cost after the last fruit).\n\n---\n\n### Complexity analysis\n\n- For each `i` we compute the transition in O(1).  \n- Loop runs `n` times (`n ≤ 1000`).  \n- **Time:** O(n)  \n- **Space:** O(n) for the dp array (can be reduced to O(1) but not required).\n\n---\n\n### Correctness proof  \n\nWe prove by reverse induction on `i` that `dp[i]` equals the minimum cost to acquire fruits `i … n-1`.\n\n**Lemma 1 (Base case).**  \n`dp[n] = 0` is correct because there are no fruits left.\n\n**Induction hypothesis.**  \nAssume for some `i+1` (`0 ≤ i+1 ≤ n`) that `dp[i+1]` is the optimal cost for the suffix starting at `i+1`.\n\n**Induction step.**  \nConsider fruit `i`. There are exactly two mutually exclusive ways to obtain it in any optimal strategy:\n\n1. **Take it for free** from a previously purchased fruit. Then the remaining work is exactly the suffix `i+1 … n-1`, costing `dp[i+1]` by the hypothesis.\n\n2. **Purchase it** at price `prices[i]`. The offer then lets us take the next `i+1` fruits (indices `i+1 … i+i+1`) for free. The first fruit whose cost is still undetermined is `i+i+2`. By the induction hypothesis the optimal cost from there onward is `dp[i+i+2]`. Total cost of this option is `prices[i] + dp[i+i+2]`.\n\nNo other possibilities exist: fruit `i` is either free (must be covered by an earlier purchase, already accounted for by the first case) or paid for (case 2). Therefore the optimal cost for the suffix starting at `i` is the minimum of the two options, which matches the recurrence defining `dp[i]`.\n\nBy induction, `dp[i]` is optimal for all `i`, and `dp[0]` is the answer for the whole array.\n\n---\n\n### Reference implementation (Python 3)\n\n```python\ndef minCoins(prices):\n    n = len(prices)\n    # dp[i] = min cost for suffix starting at i\n    dp = [0] * (n + 1)          # dp[n] = 0 already\n    # iterate from right to left\n    for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):\n        # option 1: take fruit i for free\n        take_free = dp[i + 1]\n\n        # option 2: pay for fruit i, skip the next i+1 fruits\n        skip = i + (i + 1) + 1          # = 2*i + 2\n        if skip > n:                    # out of bounds → treat as dp[n] = 0\n            pay = prices[i]\n        else:\n            pay = prices[i] + dp[skip]\n\n        dp[i] = min(take_free, pay)\n\n    return dp[0]\n```\n\nThe function follows exactly the algorithm proven correct above and conforms to the required O(n) time and O(n) space constraints.",
