    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_277",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nTakahashi is displaying a sentence with N words in a window.\r\nAll words have the same height, and the width of the i-th word (1\\leq i\\leq N) is L _ i.\nThe words are displayed in the window separated by a space of width 1.\r\nMore precisely, when the sentence is displayed in a window of width W, the following conditions are satisfied.\n\n- The sentence is divided into several lines.\n- The first word is displayed at the beginning of the top line.\n- The i-th word (2\\leq i\\leq N) is displayed either with a gap of 1 after the (i-1)-th word, or at the beginning of the line below the line containing the (i-1)-th word. It will not be displayed anywhere else.\n- The width of each line does not exceed W. Here, the width of a line refers to the distance from the left end of the leftmost word to the right end of the rightmost word.\n\nWhen Takahashi displayed the sentence in the window, the sentence fit into M or fewer lines.\r\nFind the minimum possible width of the window.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN M\r\nL _ 1 L _ 2 \\ldots L _ N\n\nOutput\n\nPrint the answer in one line.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 1\\leq M\\leq N\\leq2\\times10 ^ 5\n- 1\\leq L _ i\\leq10^9\\ (1\\leq i\\leq N)\n- All input values are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n13 3\r\n9 5 2 7 1 8 8 2 1 5 2 3 6\n\nSample Output 1\n\n26\r\n\nWhen the width of the window is 26, you can fit the given sentence into three lines as follows.\n\nYou cannot fit the given sentence into three lines when the width of the window is 25 or less, so print 26.\nNote that you should not display a word across multiple lines, let the width of a line exceed the width of the window, or rearrange the words.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n10 1\r\n1000000000 1000000000 1000000000 1000000000 1000000000 1000000000 1000000000 1000000000 1000000000 1000000000\n\nSample Output 2\n\n10000000009\r\n\nNote that the answer may not fit into a 32\\operatorname{{bit}} integer.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n30 8\r\n8 55 26 97 48 37 47 35 55 5 17 62 2 60 23 99 73 34 75 7 46 82 84 29 41 32 31 52 32 60\n\nSample Output 3\n\n189",
    "prediction": "MiniMax-M3",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**Solution Explanation**\n\nFor each word `i` we know its width `L[i]`.\nIf two consecutive words are placed on the same line, a space of width `1`\nmust be inserted between them.\nSo a consecutive block\n\n```\ni, i+1, ..., j   ( i ≤ j )\n```\n\nfits on one line iff\n\n```\nL[i] + (L[i+1]+1) + (L[i+2]+1) + … + (L[j]+1) ≤ W\n```\n\ni.e.\n\n```\n(L[i] + L[i+1] + … + L[j]) + (j-i) ≤ W                (1)\n```\n\nFor a fixed `W` we can greedily start a new line whenever (1) would be\nviolated.  \nBecause we always put a word on the current line when it still fits,\nthe greedy algorithm uses the **minimum possible number of lines**.\nIf that minimum is ≤ `M`, the width `W` is feasible.\n\nThe answer is the smallest feasible `W`.  \nBoth `W` and the check are monotone, therefore we binary‑search `W`.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Greedy check `possible(W)`\n\n```\nlines = 1                     # we will use at least one line\nline_width = 0                # sum of word widths on the current line\ni = 0\nwhile i < N:\n    # width if we put word i at the beginning of a fresh line\n    need = L[i]\n    # width if we continue the current line\n    if line_width > 0:\n        need = line_width + 1 + L[i]    # previous words + one space + new word\n\n    if need <= W:\n        line_width = need\n        i += 1\n    else:\n        # start a new line\n        lines += 1\n        line_width = L[i]   # word i begins the new line\n        i += 1\nreturn (lines ≤ M)\n```\n\n`lines ≤ 2·10⁵`, the loop is linear – `O(N)` time.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Binary search\n\n* Lower bound `lo = max(L[i])` (any width must be at least the widest word)\n* Upper bound `hi = sum(L) + (N-1)` (everything on one line, spaces included)\n\n```\nwhile lo < hi:\n    mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n    if possible(mid):\n        hi = mid\n    else:\n        lo = mid + 1\nanswer = lo\n```\n\nThe interval size halves each iteration, at most `⌈log₂(hi-lo+1)⌉ ≤ 60`\niterations because the upper bound fits into 64‑bit signed integer.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Correctness Proof  \n\nWe prove that the algorithm outputs the minimal width `W` for which the\nsentence fits into at most `M` lines.\n\n---\n\n##### Lemma 1 (Greedy optimality)\nFor a fixed window width `W`, the greedy procedure described above uses the\nminimum possible number of lines.\n\n**Proof.**  \nConsider the greedy construction line by line.\nSuppose it has already fixed the first `k-1` lines and is now deciding\nwhere to place word `p` (the first word not yet placed).\nAll feasible placements of word `p` are:\n\n* on the current line, if the width would not exceed `W`;\n* on a new line, otherwise.\n\nIf greedy puts `p` on the current line, any other feasible solution can\nalso put `p` there (otherwise the greedy choice would be illegal).  \nThus the set of words placed after `p` is unchanged or becomes *larger*\nin any alternative solution. By induction on the line count, greedy never\nuses more lines than any other feasible arrangement. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 2 (Monotonicity)\nIf a width `W` is feasible (the sentence fits into at most `M` lines),\nthen any larger width `W' > W` is also feasible.\n\n**Proof.**  \nIncreasing the width can only make the greedy check succeed more often,\nso the number of lines used by the greedy algorithm cannot increase.\nHence feasibility is preserved. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 3 (Feasibility test correctness)\nFor any `W`, `possible(W)` returns *True* iff the sentence can be displayed\nin a window of width `W` using at most `M` lines.\n\n**Proof.**  \n`possible(W)` implements the greedy algorithm and counts the resulting\nlines. By Lemma&nbsp;1 this count is the minimum possible number of lines\nfor width `W`. Therefore the returned comparison `lines ≤ M` is exactly the\nfeasibility condition. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 4 (Binary search invariant)\nDuring binary search the invariant holds:\n\n```\nlo  ≤  answer  ≤  hi\n```\n\nwhere `answer` is the minimal feasible width.\n\n**Proof.**  \nInitially `lo = max(L)` is not larger than any feasible width,\n`hi = sum(L)+(N-1)` is not smaller than any feasible width.\nThus the invariant holds at start.\n\nAssume it holds before an iteration.\nLet `mid = (lo+hi)//2`.\n\n*If `possible(mid)` is True*: by Lemma&nbsp;3 `mid` is feasible,\nso `answer ≤ mid`. We set `hi = mid`; the invariant still holds.\n\n*If `possible(mid)` is False*: `mid` is infeasible, therefore\n`answer > mid`. We set `lo = mid+1`; again the invariant holds. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 5 (Termination)\nThe binary search terminates with `lo = hi = answer`.\n\n**Proof.**  \nThe interval `[lo,hi]` strictly shrinks each iteration (`hi-mid < hi-lo`\nor `mid+1-lo < hi-lo`). Because its size is finite, the loop ends.\nWhen it ends we have `lo = hi`; by Lemma&nbsp;4 this common value equals\n`answer`. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Theorem (Overall correctness)\nThe algorithm outputs the minimum possible width of the window such that\nthe sentence can be displayed in at most `M` lines.\n\n**Proof.**  \nBy Lemma&nbsp;5 the algorithm returns `answer`.  \nFrom Lemma&nbsp;4 `answer` is feasible, and any width `< answer` is\ninfeasible. Hence `answer` is exactly the minimum feasible width. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Complexity Analysis  \n\n*Greedy check*: `O(N)` time, `O(1)` extra memory.  \n*Binary search*: at most `≈ 60` iterations → `O(N log MaxW)` time.  \n\n```\nTime   :  O(N · log ΣL)  ≤  2·10⁵ · 60  ≈ 1.2·10⁷ operations\nMemory :  O(1)   (aside from the input array)\n```\n\nBoth satisfy the limits easily.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Reference Implementation  (Python 3)\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve() -> None:\n    data = sys.stdin.read().strip().split()\n    if not data:\n        return\n    it = iter(data)\n    N = int(next(it))\n    M = int(next(it))\n    L = [int(next(it)) for _ in range(N)]\n\n    # ---------- feasibility test (greedy) ----------\n    def possible(W: int) -> bool:\n        lines = 1\n        line_width = 0  # width occupied on the current line (including spaces)\n        i = 0\n        while i < N:\n            w = L[i]\n            # width if we put this word after the previous one (with a space)\n            if line_width > 0:\n                need = line_width + 1 + w\n            else:\n                need = w\n            if need <= W:\n                line_width = need\n                i += 1\n            else:\n                # start a new line\n                lines += 1\n                if lines > M:          # early exit\n                    return False\n                line_width = w         # word begins the new line\n                i += 1\n        return lines <= M\n\n    # ---------- binary search ----------\n    lo = max(L)                       # at least the widest single word\n    # upper bound: everything on one line, spaces between all words\n    hi = sum(L) + (N - 1)\n\n    while lo < hi:\n        mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n        if possible(mid):\n            hi = mid\n        else:\n            lo = mid + 1\n\n    print(lo)\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n```\n\nThe program follows exactly the algorithm proven correct above\nand conforms to the required input / output format.",
