      "generated_answer": "**Solution Explanation**\n\nFor a fixed subsequence `S` of length `k` its power is\n\n```\npower(S) = min_{i<j} |S[i] – S[j]|\n```\n\nWe need the sum of this value over **all** subsequences of length `k`.\n\nA brute force enumeration of subsequences is `O( 2^n )` – far too slow for `n = 50`.\n\nThe crucial observation is that the power of a subsequence depends only on the **order** of its elements, not on the original positions.  \nIf we sort the whole array, any subsequence taken from the sorted array is also sorted, therefore the absolute differences become simple differences:\n\n```\nsorted subsequence: a1 < a2 < … < ak\npower = min_{i<j} (aj – ai) = min_{i=2..k} (ai – a_{i-1})\n```\n\nConsequences\n\n1. After sorting we only have to deal with **increasing** subsequences.\n2. The power of a sorted subsequence is the smallest *adjacent* gap inside it.\n\nNow we must count, over all increasing subsequences of length `k`, how many times each adjacent gap is the *minimum* gap of the subsequence.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 1.   Counting subsequences where a given gap is the minimum\n\nConsider the sorted array `v[0] < v[1] < … < v[n‑1]`.  \nPick a pair `(i, j)` with `i < j` and define the gap  \n\n```\ngap = v[j] – v[i]          (j > i)\n```\n\nWe want all increasing subsequences of length `k`\n\n```\n… < a < v[i] < v[j] < b < …\n```\n\nsuch that `v[j] – v[i]` is the **smallest** adjacent difference in the subsequence.\n\nLet  \n\n```\nL = i   (number of elements left of v[i])\nR = n-1-j (number of elements right of v[j])\n```\n\nInside the subsequence we already have the two elements `v[i]` and `v[j]`.  \nWe still have to pick\n\n* `p` elements from the left side (`0 ≤ p ≤ L`)\n* `q` elements from the right side (`0 ≤ q ≤ R`)\n\nwith the total length condition\n\n```\np + 2 + q = k   →   p + q = k-2\n```\n\nFor a fixed `p` (`0 ≤ p ≤ min(L, k-2)`) we choose `p` elements from the `L` left ones and `k-2-p` elements from the `R` right ones. The number of ways is\n\n```\nC(L, p) * C(R, k-2-p)\n```\n\nThe gap `v[j] – v[i]` will be the *minimum* iff **no** pair of consecutive chosen elements from the left side is closer together, and similarly on the right side.  \n\nBecause the whole array is sorted, any two consecutive elements on the left side are at least as far apart as `v[i] – v[i-1]`, and the same holds on the right side with `v[j+1] – v[j]`.  \nTherefore the condition \"`gap` is the minimum\" is automatically satisfied **as long as we do not pick two elements that are *strictly* closer than `gap`**.\n\nConsequently we must *exclude* from the left side those elements that are too close to `v[i]`, and from the right side those that are too close to `v[j]`.\n\nDefine\n\n```\nLclose = number of indices t < i with   v[i] - v[t] < gap\nRclose = number of indices t > j with   v[t] - v[j] < gap\n```\n\nOnly the elements **outside** these \"too close\" zones may be used in a valid subsequence.  \nLet\n\n```\nLgood = L   - Lclose          (available elements on the left)\nRgood = R   - Rclose          (available elements on the right)\n```\n\nNow the count of subsequences where `(i, j)` contributes the gap `gap` as the minimum is exactly\n\n```\nsum_{p=0}^{min(Lgood, k-2)}  C(Lgood, p) * C(Rgood, k-2-p)\n```\n\nAll other possibilities (using an element from the `close` zones) would create a smaller adjacent difference and are discarded.\n\nThe contribution of the pair `(i, j)` to the final answer is therefore\n\n```\ngap *  ( number of valid subsequences using (i,j) as the minimum gap )\n```\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 2.   Computing `Lclose` and `Rclose` efficiently\n\nThe total number of pairs `(i, j)` is `O(n²)` ( ≤ 1225 for `n = 50`).  \nFor each pair we can compute `Lclose` and `Rclose` in `O(n)` time by scanning left and right, leading to `O(n³)` which is fine ( ≤ 125 000 operations).\n\nA more elegant `O(n²)` solution uses two‑pointer sweeps, but with the tiny constraints the cubic solution is simpler and well within limits.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 3.   Modulo arithmetic\n\nAll results must be taken modulo `M = 10⁹+7`.  \nWe pre‑compute binomial coefficients `C(n, r) (0 ≤ n ≤ 50)` using Pascal's triangle.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 4.   Algorithm\n\n```\n1. Sort the input array → v[0..n-1]\n2. Pre‑compute binomial coefficients C[n][r] modulo M.\n3. ans = 0\n4. For every pair (i, j) with 0 ≤ i < j < n:\n       gap = v[j] - v[i]\n       // count elements to the left/right\n       L = i\n       R = n-1-j\n       Lclose = number of t < i with v[i] - v[t] < gap\n       Rclose = number of t > j with v[t] - v[j] < gap\n       Lgood = L - Lclose\n       Rgood = R - Rclose\n       total = 0\n       need = k-2\n       for p = 0 … min(Lgood, need):\n            q = need - p\n            if 0 ≤ q ≤ Rgood:\n                 total += C[Lgood][p] * C[Rgood][q]   (mod M)\n       ans = (ans + gap % M * total) % M\n5. Return ans\n```\n\nAll arithmetic uses modulo `M`.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 5.   Correctness Proof  \n\nWe prove that the algorithm returns the required sum of powers.\n\n---\n\n##### Lemma 1  \nFor any subsequence `S` of length `k`, after sorting the whole array the subsequence consisting of the same elements (now in increasing order) has the same power as `S`.\n\n**Proof.**  \nSorting does not change the multiset of elements, only their order. For a multiset the minimum absolute difference depends only on the values, not on the original positions. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 2  \nFor a sorted increasing subsequence `a₁ < a₂ < … < a_k` its power equals  \n`min_{2 ≤ t ≤ k} (a_t – a_{t‑1})`.\n\n**Proof.**  \nAll pairs in the subsequence are ordered. The smallest possible difference among any pair of distinct elements is achieved by two consecutive elements in the sorted order. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 3  \nFix a pair of indices `i < j` in the sorted array, let `gap = v[j] – v[i]`.  \nA sorted subsequence of length `k` that contains both `v[i]` and `v[j]` has `gap` as its minimum adjacent difference **iff**\n\n* all elements left of `v[i]` that are chosen are from the set  \n  `{ t | t < i  and  v[i] – v[t] ≥ gap }`,\n* all elements right of `v[j]` that are chosen are from the set  \n  `{ t | t > j  and  v[t] – v[j] ≥ gap }`.\n\n**Proof.**  \n*If part.*  \nAssume the chosen left/right elements satisfy the distance condition. Then any two consecutive elements on the left differ by at least `gap`; the same holds on the right. The only adjacent pair that could be smaller than `gap` would involve one of the selected elements together with `v[i]` or `v[j]`. By construction those differences are exactly `gap` or larger. Therefore the smallest adjacent gap in the whole subsequence is `gap`.\n\n*Only‑if part.*  \nConversely, suppose a subsequence uses an element `v[t]` on the left with `v[i] – v[t] < gap`. Then the adjacent pair `(v[t], v[i])` is smaller than `gap`, contradicting the assumption that `gap` is the minimum. The same argument applies to the right side. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 4  \nFor a fixed pair `(i, j)` let `Lgood` be the number of indices left of `i` whose distance to `v[i]` is **not** smaller than `gap`, and analogously `Rgood` for the right side.  \nThe number of length‑`k` subsequences whose minimum adjacent gap equals `gap` and which contain `v[i]` and `v[j]` is  \n\n```\n Σ_{p=0}^{min(Lgood, k-2)}  C(Lgood, p) * C(Rgood, k-2-p)\n```\n\n**Proof.**  \nA valid subsequence must contain the two mandatory elements `v[i]`, `v[j]`.  \nWe need to select `p` further elements from the",
