      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to determine if all indices in the array are in the same connected component when edges are formed between indices with `gcd > 1`.\n\n**Approach: Union-Find with Prime Factor Grouping**\n\nBuilding a graph directly would require O(n²) edges, which is too slow. Instead, I can group indices by their prime factors and use Union-Find to efficiently connect them.\n\n**Key insight:** Two numbers share a common factor (gcd > 1) if and only if they share at least one prime factor. So indices sharing any prime factor should be unioned together.\n\n**Algorithm:**\n1. For each index, find all prime factors of `nums[i]`\n2. Use a dictionary mapping each prime factor to the first index that contains it\n3. Union the current index with that stored index for each shared prime\n4. Finally, check if all indices belong to the same connected component\n\n```python\ndef canTraverseAllPairs(nums):\n    n = len(nums)\n    \n    if n == 0:\n        return True\n    \n    parent = list(range(n))\n    \n    def find(x):\n        root = x\n        while parent[root] != root:\n            root = parent[root]\n        while parent[x] != root:\n            parent[x], x = root, parent[x]\n        return root\n    \n    def union(x, y):\n        root_x = find(x)\n        root_y = find(y)\n        if root_x != root_y:\n            parent[root_x] = root_y\n    \n    def get_prime_factors(num):\n        factors = set()\n        d = 2\n        while d * d <= num:\n            if num % d == 0:\n                factors.add(d)\n                while num % d == 0:\n                    num //= d\n            d += 1\n        if num > 1:\n            factors.add(num)\n        return factors\n    \n    prime_to_index = {}\n    \n    for i in range(n):\n        for p in get_prime_factors(nums[i]):\n            if p in prime_to_index:\n                union(i, prime_to_index[p])\n            else:\n                prime_to_index[p] = i\n    \n    root = find(0)\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        if find(i) != root:\n            return False\n    \n    return True\n```\n\n**Why this works:**\n- Any two indices with a shared prime factor get unioned (transitively through the prime factor mapping)\n- Using path compression in `find` keeps operations near O(1) amortized\n- Each index contributes at most O(log nums[i]) prime factors, making the total work O(n · log(max_value) · α(n)), which easily handles the constraints of n ≤ 10⁵ and nums[i] ≤ 10⁵\n\n**Trace through examples:**\n- `[2,3,6]`: 0→{2}, 1→{3}, 2→{2,3} → 0 and 2 share factor 2, 1 and 2 share factor 3 → all connected ✓\n- `[3,9,5]`: 0→{3}, 1→{3}, 2→{5} → 0 and 1 share factor 3, but 2 has only {5}, no connection → not all connected ✗\n- `[4,3,12,8]`: 0→{2}, 1→{3}, 2→{2,3}, 3→{2} → all connected through shared factors ✓",
