      "generated_answer": "\nTo solve this problem, we can model it as a graph traversal problem where each index in the array represents a node, and there is an edge between two nodes if the greatest common divisor (gcd) of their corresponding values is greater than 1. The task is to determine if every pair of nodes is connected directly or indirectly (i.e., there's a path between them).\n\nFor this to be true, the graph must be connected. We can use a union-find data structure (also known as a disjoint set data structure) to keep track of connected components in the graph. The idea is to group nodes that are directly connected, and if all nodes are in a single group after processing all edges, then the graph is connected, and we return `True`. Otherwise, we return `False`.\n\nHere's the code for the function:\n\n```python\nfrom math import gcd\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\ndef possibleTraverse(nums):\n    # Build a mapping from prime factors to the indices that contain them\n    factor_to_indices = defaultdict(list)\n    for index, num in enumerate(nums):\n        for factor in range(2, int(num**0.5) + 1):\n            if num % factor == 0:\n                factor_to_indices[factor].append(index)\n                while num % factor == 0:\n                    num //= factor\n        if num > 1:  # If there's a prime factor larger than sqrt(num)\n            factor_to_indices[num].append(index)\n\n    # Union-Find data structure to keep track of connected components\n    parent = list(range(len(nums)))\n\n    def find(x):\n        if parent[x] != x:\n            parent[x] = find(parent[x])\n        return parent[x]\n\n    def union(x, y):\n        rootX = find(x)\n        rootY = find(y)\n        if rootX != rootY:\n            parent[rootY] = rootX\n\n    # Union all indices that share at least one common prime factor\n    for indices in factor_to_indices.values():\n        first_index = indices[0]\n        for index in indices[1:]:\n            union(first_index, index)\n\n    # Check if all nodes are in the same connected component\n    root = find(0)\n    for index in range(1, len(nums)):\n        if find(index) != root:\n            return False\n    return True\n```\n\nThe function `possibleTraverse` takes the list of integers `nums` as input and returns a boolean value indicating whether it's possible to traverse between all pairs of indices as per the problem statement. The function doesn't invoke itself but will execute when called with a list of integers.",
