    "prediction": "MiniMax-M3",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**Solution Explanation**\n\nFor each element of `nums` we only need to know whether it is prime or not.\nSince `nums[i] ≤ 100`, we can pre‑compute the set of primes up to 100.\nAfter that we scan the array, remember the **first** index of a prime and the\n**last** index of a prime.  \nIf there is at least one prime (guaranteed by the statement) the answer is\n`last - first`.\n\nThe algorithm runs in linear time `O(n)` and uses `O(1)` extra space.\n\n\n\n```python\ndef maximumPrimeDistance(nums):\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns the maximum distance between the indices of two (not necessarily\n    different) prime numbers in the array nums.\n\n    Parameters\n    ----------\n    nums : List[int]\n        Input array, 1 <= len(nums) <= 3*10^5, each element 1 <= nums[i] <= 100.\n\n    Returns\n    -------\n    int\n        Maximum absolute index difference between any two prime positions.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    # ---------- 1. pre‑compute primes up to 100 ----------\n    MAX_VAL = 100\n    is_prime = [True] * (MAX_VAL + 1)\n    is_prime[0] = is_prime[1] = False\n    for p in range(2, int(MAX_VAL ** 0.5) + 1):\n        if is_prime[p]:\n            for multiple in range(p * p, MAX_VAL + 1, p):\n                is_prime[multiple] = False\n\n    # ---------- 2. scan the array ----------\n    first_prime = -1   # earliest index of a prime\n    last_prime  = -1   # latest   index of a prime\n\n    for i, val in enumerate(nums):\n        if is_prime[val]:\n            if first_prime == -1:          # first time we meet a prime\n                first_prime = i\n            last_prime = i                 # always update the last one seen\n\n    # The problem guarantees at least one prime, so first_prime != -1.\n    return last_prime - first_prime\n```\n\n**Explanation of the Code**\n\n1. **Prime table** – The sieve of Eratosthenes fills `is_prime[x]` with `True`\n   iff `x` is a prime number (`0 ≤ x ≤ 100`).  \n   This work is independent of the input size and runs in constant time.\n\n2. **Scanning** – While iterating over `nums` we keep two positions:\n   - `first_prime`: the smallest index where a prime occurs,\n   - `last_prime`: the greatest index where a prime occurs.\n\n   Whenever we encounter a prime we set `first_prime` (if it hasn't been set)\n   and always update `last_prime`.\n\n3. **Result** – The maximum possible distance is achieved by the farthest\n   apart primes, i.e. the ones at `first_prime` and `last_prime`. Their\n   distance is `last_prime - first_prime`.\n\nThe function complies with the required constraints and runs in linear time\nwith respect to the length of `nums`.",
