      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem:\n- A string has length 3-100\n- All chars except one are the same\n- Need to find the 1-based position of the unique character\n\nSince exactly one character differs from the rest, I can find it by checking pairs:\n- If S[0] == S[1], the unique char must be elsewhere (compare against S[0])\n- Otherwise, S[0] or S[1] is the unique one (compare S[0] with S[2])\n\n```python\ndef solve():\n    import sys\n    S = sys.stdin.readline().strip()\n    \n    # If first two chars are the same, the unique one must differ from them\n    if S[0] == S[1]:\n        target = S[0]\n        for i in range(len(S)):\n            if S[i] != target:\n                print(i + 1)\n                return\n    # Otherwise, S[0] and S[1] are different, so S[0] is unique (since most chars are the same)\n    else:\n        # Check S[2] to determine which of S[0], S[1] is unique\n        if S[0] == S[2]:\n            print(2)\n        else:\n            print(1)\n\nsolve()\n```\n\n**How it works:**\n- **Case 1**: `S[0] == S[1]` → Both are the majority character, so scan for the differing one.\n- **Case 2**: `S[0] != S[1]` → Exactly one of them is the unique character. Compare with `S[2]` (which must be the majority) to decide:\n  - If `S[0] == S[2]` → `S[1]` is unique (position 2)\n  - Else → `S[0]` is unique (position 1)\n\n**Verification with samples:**\n- `yay`: S[0]='y', S[1]='a' differ → S[2]='y' equals S[0] → answer is **2** ✓\n- `egg`: S[0]='e', S[1]='g' differ → S[2]='g' equals S[1] → answer is **1** ✓\n- `zzzzzwz`: S[0]=S[1]='z' → scan finds 'w' at index 5 → answer is **6** ✓",
