    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "\nTo solve this problem, we need to find the position of the character in a string that differs from all other characters. The string is guaranteed to consist of two distinct lowercase English letters, with all but one character being the same. \n\n### Approach\n1. **Problem Analysis**: The problem requires identifying the single character in the string that is different from all other characters. Given the constraints, the string will always have exactly two distinct characters, with one appearing only once and the other appearing the remaining number of times.\n2. **Intuition**: The solution leverages the fact that the different character must appear exactly once. By checking the count of the first character in the string, we can determine if it is the different character (appearing once) or if we need to find the first occurrence of the other character (which will appear more than once).\n3. **Algorithm Selection**: The algorithm involves:\n   - Counting the occurrences of the first character in the string.\n   - If the first character appears exactly once, its position (1-indexed) is the answer.\n   - Otherwise, the first occurrence of the other character (which differs from the first character) is the answer.\n4. **Complexity Analysis**: The solution involves a single pass through the string to count the occurrences of the first character and another pass to find the first differing character if necessary. The overall complexity is O(n), where n is the length of the string, which is efficient given the constraints.\n\n### Solution Code\n```python\ndef main():\n    S = input().strip()\n    n = len(S)\n    if S.count(S[0]) == 1:\n        print(1)\n    else:\n        for i in range(1, n):\n            if S[i] != S[0]:\n                print(i + 1)\n                break\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    main()\n```\n\n### Explanation\n1. **Reading Input**: The input string is read from standard input.\n2. **Counting First Character**: The code checks how many times the first character appears in the string.\n3. **Decision Making**: \n   - If the first character appears exactly once, its position (1) is printed immediately.\n   - If the first character appears more than once, the code scans the string starting from the second character to find the first occurrence of a different character. The position of this character (1-indexed) is then printed.\n4. **Output**: The solution efficiently locates and prints the position of the differing character, leveraging the problem constraints to minimize unnecessary computations.\n\nThis approach ensures that we quickly identify the differing character by leveraging the known structure of the input string, providing an optimal solution with minimal computational overhead.",
