      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to find the **maximum** number of jumps to reach the last index. Since I want maximum jumps, I should prefer taking the smallest possible jumps (least gain in position).\n\n## Approach: DP with Binary Search\n\nFor each index `i`, I want to transition to the next possible index `j` where:\n- `nums[i] - target <= nums[j] <= nums[i] + target`\n- `j > i`\n\n**Key insight for maximum jumps:** Among all valid next indices, I want to jump to the one with the **smallest** position (closest to `i`).\n\n### Implementation Steps:\n1. `dp[i]` = max jumps from `i` to `n-1`\n2. For each `i`, find all `j > i` where `nums[i] - target <= nums[j] <= nums[i] + target`\n3. Pick the smallest valid `j`, giving `dp[i] = dp[j] + 1`\n4. If no valid `j` exists with reachable path, `dp[i] = -1`\n\nI use binary search on a sorted list of `(nums[idx], idx)` pairs to efficiently find valid ranges:\n- `left`: smallest index in `[nums[i]-target, nums[i]+target]` with position > i\n- `right`: largest index in that range with position > i\n\n```python\nfrom sortedcontainers import SortedList\n\nclass Solution:\n    def maximumJumps(self, nums: List[int], target: int) -> int:\n        n = len(nums)\n        dp = [float('inf')] * n\n        dp[n - 1] = 0  # No jumps needed to stay at last index\n        \n        # Store pairs of (nums[idx], idx) and maintain sorted order by nums value\n        sorted_pairs = SortedList([(nums[n - 1], n - 1)])\n        \n        # Iterate from right to left\n        for i in range(n - 2, -1, -1):\n            lo = nums[i] - target\n            hi = nums[i] + target\n            \n            # Find indices j in [lo, hi] using binary search\n            left_pos = sorted_pairs.bisect_left((lo, -1))\n            right_pos = sorted_pairs.bisect_right((hi, n))\n            \n            min_jumps = float('inf')\n            # Iterate through all candidates in range (at most O(n) but avg case small)\n            for k in range(left_pos, right_pos):\n                _, j = sorted_pairs[k]\n                if dp[j] != float('inf'):\n                    min_jumps = min(min_jumps, dp[j] + 1)\n            \n            dp[i] = min_jumps if min_jumps != float('inf') else -1\n            sorted_pairs.add((nums[i], i))\n        \n        return dp[0] if dp[0] != float('inf') else -1\n```\n\n### Cleaner Final Version (avoiding external dependency):\n\n```python\nfrom typing import List\n\nclass Solution:\n    def maximumJumps(self, nums: List[int], target: int) -> int:\n        n = len(nums)\n        dp = [float('inf')] * n\n        dp[n - 1] = 0\n        \n        # Maintain list of (value, index) sorted by value\n        sorted_vals = [(nums[n - 1], n - 1)]\n        \n        def insert_sorted(value, index):\n            # Binary search to find insertion point\n            lo, hi = 0, len(sorted_vals)\n            while lo < hi:\n                mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n                if sorted_vals[mid][0] < value:\n                    lo = mid + 1\n                else:\n                    hi = mid\n            sorted_vals.insert(lo, (value, index))\n        \n        def find_range(lo_val, hi_val):\n            \"\"\"Return indices in sorted_vals where lo_val <= value <= hi_val\"\"\"\n            # Left bound\n            l, r = 0, len(sorted_vals)\n            while l < r:\n                mid = (l + r) // 2\n                if sorted_vals[mid][0] < lo_val:\n                    l = mid + 1\n                else:\n                    r = mid\n            left = l\n            # Right bound\n            l, r = left, len(sorted_vals)\n            while l < r:\n                mid = (l + r) // 2\n                if sorted_vals[mid][0] <= hi_val:\n                    l = mid + 1\n                else:\n                    r = mid\n            return left, r\n        \n        for i in range(n - 2, -1, -1):\n            lo = nums[i] - target\n            hi = nums[i] + target\n            left, right = find_range(lo, hi)\n            \n            min_jumps = float('inf')\n            for k in range(left, right):\n                _, j = sorted_vals[k]\n                if j > i and dp[j] != float('inf'):\n                    min_jumps = min(min_jumps, dp[j] + 1)\n            \n            dp[i] = min_jumps if min_jumps != float('inf') else -1\n            insert_sorted(nums[i], i)\n        \n        return dp[0] if dp[0] != float('inf') else -1\n```\n\n### Most Efficient Version with `SortedList`:\n\n```python\nfrom typing import List\nfrom sortedcontainers import SortedList\n\nclass Solution:\n    def maximumJumps(self, nums: List[int], target: int) -> int:\n        n = len(nums)\n        dp = [float('inf')] * n\n        dp[n - 1] = 0\n        \n        # SortedList maintains (value, index) in sorted order\n        sorted_pairs = SortedList([(nums[n - 1], n - 1)])\n        \n        for i in range(n - 2, -1, -1):\n            lo = nums[i] - target\n            hi = nums[i] + target\n            \n            # Get iterator over all valid pairs in range\n            candidates = sorted_pairs.irange((lo, -float('inf')), \n                                              (hi, float('inf')))\n            \n            min_jumps = float('inf')\n            for val, j in candidates:\n                if j > i and dp[j] != float('inf'):\n                    min_jumps = min(min_jumps, dp[j] + 1)\n            \n            dp[i] = min_jumps if min_jumps != float('inf') else -1\n            sorted_pairs.add((nums[i], i))\n        \n        return dp[0] if dp[0] != float('inf') else -1\n```\n\n### Complexity:\n- **Time:** `O(n²)` worst case, but typically much faster with `SortedList` due to efficient range queries\n- **Space:** `O(n)` for the DP and sorted structure\n\nThe algorithm processes indices from right to left, maintaining a sorted structure for efficient binary search of valid next indices, then computes `dp[i]` based on the minimum reachable `dp[j] + 1` among all valid candidates.",
