    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Statement 1| A U2F USB dongle prevents malware on the user’s computer from stealing the user’s second factor to authenticate as that user even when the user’s computer is turned off. Statement 2| A server using U2F can reliably determine that the user who is attempting to login is indeed behind the computer that sent the login request.\n\nOptions: \nA. False, True\nB. True, True\nC. False, False\nD. Both statements are partially true\nE. False, Not enough information\nF. Not enough information, Not enough information\nG. True, False\nH. True, Not enough information\nI. Not enough information, True\nJ. Not enough information, False\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "MiniMax-M3",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to analyze each statement about U2F (Universal 2nd Factor) authentication.\n\n**Statement 1:** A U2F USB dongle prevents malware from stealing the second factor even when the computer is off.\n- U2F uses challenge-response where the private key never leaves the dongle. Malware on the computer cannot extract the key, so it cannot steal the second factor. ✓ **True**\n\n**Statement 2:** A server can reliably determine the user is behind the computer that sent the login request.\n- U2F verifies that the legitimate dongle was used (proving possession of the second factor), but it cannot verify the physical presence of the user. Malware on the computer could trick the user into authenticating, or someone with access to an unlocked session could use the dongle. The server only knows \"this dongle was tapped,\" not \"this human is present.\" ✗ **False**\n\nThe correct answer is **G. True, False**.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
