    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_5765",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: One aspect of Marxian economics is that some of the pre-dictions of Marx were proven wrong. As a result of this, some radical economists have only adopted the tools of Marxian analysis, without necessarily accepting Marx's con-clusions . In the nineteenth century, other Marxists became 'revisionists'. Explain briefly what distinguishes a re-visionist from a Marxist.\n\nOptions: \nA. Revisionists maintain that capitalism will naturally evolve into socialism without any intervention\nB. Revisionists support the idea of a permanent dictatorship of the proletariat as the path to socialism\nC. Revisionists focus on individual enlightenment and moral reform as the means to achieve socialism\nD. Revisionists argue for immediate abolition of the state to achieve socialism\nE. Revisionists believe in gradual evolution towards socialism through political action and trade unionism, rejecting violent class struggle.\nF. Revisionists emphasize the importance of international trade as a primary tool for achieving socialism\nG. Revisionists adhere strictly to Marx's labor theory of value\nH. Revisionists reject the concept of class struggle entirely and believe in maintaining the status quo\nI. Revisionists fully accept Marx's theories and predictions\nJ. Revisionists believe in achieving socialism through violent revolution\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "MiniMax-M3",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The question asks what distinguishes a revisionist from a Marxist in the context of nineteenth-century socialism. Revisionism, particularly associated with Eduard Bernstein, arose as a response to the perceived failure of Marx's predictions (such as the increasing impoverishment of the proletariat and the imminent collapse of capitalism). Revisionists argued that socialism could be achieved gradually through reformist means rather than revolutionary upheaval.\n\nOption A is incorrect because revisionists didn't believe capitalism would naturally evolve into socialism without intervention—they advocated active reform.\nOption B is incorrect because revisionists specifically rejected the need for a \"permanent dictatorship of the proletariat.\"\nOption C mischaracterizes revisionism as focused on moral reform, which isn't the core distinction.\nOption D is incorrect; immediate abolition of the state is more associated with anarchism.\nOption E correctly captures Bernstein's revisionism: gradual evolution through political action and trade unionism, rejecting violent revolution.\nOptions F, G, I, and J are factually incorrect descriptions of revisionism.\nOption H is incorrect because revisionists didn't reject class struggle entirely or maintain the status quo.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 347,
        "output_tokens": 216,
        "total_tokens": 563
      },
      "provider": "minimax",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": 0.0007265999999999999,
    "accuracy": 1.0,
    "for_optimality": true
  },
