    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The question asks why an AC motor requires variable voltage when operating on variable frequency supplies. This is a fundamental concept in variable frequency drives (VFDs).\n\nWhen the frequency supplied to an AC motor is varied, the voltage must also be varied proportionally to maintain a constant flux (V/f ratio). If the voltage remained constant while frequency decreased, the flux would increase, causing magnetic saturation of the motor's core. Conversely, if frequency increased with constant voltage, flux would decrease, reducing torque capability.\n\nTherefore, the variable voltage is required to avoid the effect of magnetic saturation in the motor's iron core.\n\n\\boxed{H}",
