      "generated_answer": "\nThe energy stored in an inductor is given by \\( E = \\frac{1}{2} L I^2 \\), but we need to integrate \\( \\frac{1}{2} \\frac{\\phi(i)}{i} i \\, di \\) from \\( i(0) \\) to \\( i(1) \\). Given \\( \\phi(t) = \\frac{1}{3} i^3(t) \\) and \\( i(t) = 2 \\sin t \\), the energy is \\( E = \\int_0^1 \\frac{1}{2} \\frac{\\frac{1}{3} (2 \\sin t)^3}{2 \\sin t} \\, dt = \\int_0^1 \\frac{1}{3} (2 \\sin t)^2 \\, dt = \\frac{4}{3} \\int_0^1 \\sin^2 t \\, dt = \\frac{4}{3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{\\pi}{2} = \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\approx 1.05 \\). The closest option is \\boxed{A}.",
