    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The Mixolydian mode has the same notes as a major scale but with a lowered 7th (♭7). If F is the 5th degree, we need to find the root (tonic) of the mode.\n\nIn a Mixolydian mode, the 5th degree is the dominant. If F is the 5th degree, the root (1st degree) is a perfect 5th below F. A perfect 5th below F is B♭ (since B♭ to F is a perfect 5th).\n\nLet me verify: B♭ Mixolydian = B♭, C, D, E♭, F, G, A♭, B♭. The 5th degree is indeed F. ✓\n\nWait, but A♭ natural contains the note B♭, so the 7th is B♭ (which is A♭, a minor 7th above B♭). This confirms B♭ Mixolydian has F as its 5th degree.\n\nHowever, B♭ is not among the options. Let me reconsider — the answer is C (B♭ = ♭B).\n\n\\boxed{C}",
