    "prediction": "MiniMax-M3",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Looking at this question, I need to evaluate which option accurately describes the shift in how instrument families were used from the Baroque to the Classical era.\n\n**Option A** correctly captures a significant shift: in the Baroque era, the continuo (harpsichord, low strings like viola da gamba, cello) played a prominent, often equal role with higher strings. By the Classical era, violins clearly dominated melodic material while lower strings (violas, cellos, basses) primarily provided harmonic and rhythmic support.\n\n**Option B** is incorrect—Classical era writing did not double strings and winds on every thematic line; this was more of a Baroque practice in some contexts.\n\n**Option C** is incorrect because bassoons did not become a standard bass-line support instrument until later (Romantic era); in the Classical era, they were often used as melodic solo instruments.\n\n**Option D** is incorrect—the relationship described is essentially reversed. Strings remained the prominent section, and woodwinds were generally used for color and melodic embellishment, not as the primary rhythmic foundation.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
