      "generated_answer": "Looking at each excerpt, I need to identify compound meter, which requires the beat to divide into three equal parts (typically with a \"3\" numerator in the meter signature, like 6/8, 9/8, 12/8).\n\n**Option A:** L:1/16, M:3/2 — Meter 3/2 is simple triple, but with L:1/16 (sixteenth note unit), each beat contains sixteenth notes that could be grouped in threes, creating compound subdivision. The rhythmic groupings with \"e2\" and note patterns suggest compound feel.\n\n**Option B:** L:1/8, M:3/2 — Contains dotted-note figures (A6, G3, A) suggesting duple/triple groupings with the eighth note as the pulse, indicating compound meter feel.\n\n**Option C:** L:1/8, M:3/2 — Uses eighth notes with patterns like \"cB\" and \"dc\" that can be grouped in compound divisions (groups of 3 eighth notes).\n\n**Option D:** L:1/8, M:3/2 — Similar to C but with additional compound-style figures like \"A3/2\" (dotted quarter) reinforcing compound subdivision.\n\nAll four excerpts demonstrate characteristics of compound meter through their rhythmic groupings and note values.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
