    "global index": "MusicTheoryBench_248",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which type of chord has all of its inversions being equivalent chords?\n\nOptions: \nA. Minor triad\nB. Diminished triad\nC. Half-diminished seventh chord\nD. Fully diminished seventh chord\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "MiniMax-M3",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to determine which chord has all inversions sounding equivalent (i.e., all inversions are the same chord due to symmetry).\n\n**Analysis of each option:**\n\n- **A. Minor triad** (e.g., C-Eb-G): Inversions are C minor, Eb major (first inversion), and G major (second inversion) — these are clearly different sonorities.\n\n- **B. Diminished triad** (e.g., C-Eb-Gb): Inversions produce a minor third and then a major third, but enharmonically the notes form the same diminished structure; however, on equal-tempered instruments the intervals differ, and the triad's inversions are enharmonically related but notationally distinct in function.\n\n- **C. Half-diminished seventh chord** (e.g., C-Eb-Gb-Bb): Contains a minor third, minor third, and major third — intervals differ across inversions, so inversions are not equivalent.\n\n- **D. Fully diminished seventh chord** (e.g., C-Eb-Gb-Bbb=A): Built entirely of stacked minor thirds. Every inversion produces the exact same interval structure (minor third + minor third + minor third), and all notes can be respelled as the root of the same chord. This is the only chord where all four inversions are truly equivalent — it is a symmetric chord.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
      "success": true,
